Track 2: Grafting the Iron Lung
- Story & Context: Silas sits in his basement workspace, carefully soldering a withered, pressed sunflower into the circuit path of his MS-10 to "teach the machine to die." His fingers are stained with oil and chlorophyll, and he refuses to leave until the synth creates a sound that mimics the exact frequency of a human sob. He whispers to the circuit board, "If I stitch your lungs to mine, we can breathe this rust together."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A close-up, macro-photograph of a wilted sunflower petal pressed against a complex nest of copper wires and vacuum tubes, soft blue and bruised purple lighting, melancholy and delicate, high detail.

Track 3: Who Taught the Concrete to Weep?
- Story & Context: Years after the district was demolished, a tourist at the memorial site found a charred patch-cord buried in the sidewalk and wondered if the music ever truly stopped. The ground beneath the site remains strangely warm to the touch, vibrating at an impossible frequency that echoes the old protest anthems of the squatters. A local drifter simply laughed when asked about it and muttered, "You’re standing on the rhythm that buried the city."
